<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">National Congress (Belgium)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National Congress was the assembly elected in November 1830, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgian-revolution">Belgian Revolution</a>, to give the new state a constitution. It declared Belgian independence, excluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a> from the throne and on 7 February 1831 adopted a liberal constitution that became a model in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After the great powers rejected a French prince, it elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</a> as King of the Belgians in June 1831.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-congress-belgium.jpg" alt="National Congress (Belgium)" width="320" height="508" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Trougnouf (Benoit Brummer) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Congress_Column,_Brussels_(DSCF3585).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
